d83a16cf (2099 1793550147637 "guenchi" (("4gm4c2jr" . 3240) ("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . section) (title . "landing f356b2") (src . "test: pids from pid files and $! verified before a fixture signals them\n\nThe second slice of the signal census. After the ps lookups, the\nsends left in the .sc fixtures were to pids read from a pid file or\nfrom $!: thirty-six in twenty-two fixtures, each sent some time\nafter the read, when the process could have ended and its pid been\nreused. They go through signal! of test/signal-send.ss, and each pid\nis registered at its first read: a $! right after the spawning call\nreturns (several of those daemons are expected to refuse and exit\nearly), a pid the process writes itself (a daemon's own pid file, a\nstub's <sk>.pid, runner.pid, an `echo $$` file) at the first read\nafter the start was awaited, before any row that may stop it. The\nperl stubs of liveness and liveness-streams bind their socket first\nand write the pid file on the next line, so the socket alone does not\nsay the file is there: the file is waited for after the socket and\nregistered then; stop-stubs! sends through signal!. daemon-e1's\nsignal-tagged! is signal! of the pid registered when the tagged\ndaemon came up. runner-self reads runner.pid through the\npid-registered! the first slice gave the file. change-stream's\ndrain-daemon!, which had no caller, goes. startup-ratio's `started`\nlist of arm labels is renamed started-labels, since the include\ndefines started; eval-lang's own alive? is the include's.\n\nGenerated shell text gets the check inline, since the program cannot\ncall signal! from inside its own shell loop. cli1's release-text\nrecords the holder's pid and its start instant in one shell text\nright after the `&` (started-text), and the kill after an expiry runs\nonly while the instant variable is non-empty, kill -0 answers and\n`ps -o lstart=` still prints that instant, the comparison and the\nkill on one line; the runner's holder writes the instant beside its\npid file and the caller compares it the same way. The USR1 loops of\nliveness and liveness-streams come from one helper, signal-loop-text:\nthe program's start instant is read once before the loop is forked,\nand each round sends only while the instant was captured and the pid\nstill has it, so a mismatch ends the loop and an empty capture sends\nnothing; $$ there is the shell system starts, not the program, so the\nform reads the pid with get-process-id and splices it.\n\nNot converted: facade-proc's library-send!, end-and-wait!'s sender\nfor the library's worker handles and the children listed while those\nare STOPped (ROUNDS-1, whose pid comes from a pid file, passes signal!\ninstead), and the SELF sends, each a named remainder; facade-proc's\nworker-kill! and daemon-idle's Popen.kill on its own unreaped child, a\ncomment each (the handle holds the identity). The perl launcher and\nrun-fixtures.sh are the third slice.\n\nTests: test/signal-audit.sc grows from 32 to 70 rows. SA-3 covers 29\nfiles: every item-5 and item-6 fixture with its remainders and its\nchecked forms (the started! and signal! counts, cli1's started-text\nand `ps -o lstart=` lines, the two signal-loop-text calls, ROUNDS-1's\n`(end-and-wait! (list pid) signal!)`), and the struck rows of the\nslice-B entries. The bare-send rule gains the spliced signal: `kill -`\nfollowed at once by `\"` is a send, whatever follows, found by this\nslice's base reading of the rule, which named daemon-e1's\n`\"kill -\" name` and nothing else (a control row reads a written-out\nsignal, a spliced one and `kill -l` as 1, 1, 0; red on the old rule).\nSA-7, new: a program counting USR1 runs signal-loop-text's form with\nPATH shimmed so that ps prints nothing and with the real ps, the shim\nthe only difference: 0 hits and 4. Red first on the base fixtures\nwith this slice's audit: 46 of 69 rows (both rows of every new or\nchanged SA-3 entry, daemon-e1's bare row through the widened rule);\nthe control row red only under the old rule, SA-7 only under a helper\nwithout the empty-capture guard. The twenty-four fixtures run alone\non the base and on the edit print the same rows, rc and fails\n(f0-ondemand's COST-1 and startup-ratio's two red on both); no \"No\nsuch process\"; new NOTE lines only in daemon-socket (three, daemons\nthat refuse to start) and change-stream-client (six, fakes already\nended). Mutants: serves-store? as a substring test reds both\nSA-6 rows; signal! without the instant comparison reds SA-4's\nidentity row; signal-loop-text without the empty-capture guard reds\nSA-7; the library-handle exemption left over ROUNDS-1 reds the\nfacade-proc entry. Reviewed in two rounds: the first found the\ngenerated loop accepting an empty start instant and ROUNDS-1's sends\nunder the library-handle exemption, both fixed; the second no\nobjection from either reviewer. rows-baseline.txt takes the new\nhashes of the touched fixtures and signal-audit's count (70 rows, 82\nlines), and the line counts of daemon-socket and change-stream-client\nas read.\n\nStage on an isolated copy of the tree (241 fixtures): only the known\npair red (f0-ondemand COST-1 over its budget, startup-ratio C1/C3);\nsignal-audit green; three python fixtures run; row baseline hashes\nall the described versions, the line counts drift on the same ten\nnames as the previous gate; no process of the run left; source\nalternation min ratio 0.9987; alternation of the compiled product\nagainst the base median 0.9991 (p10 ratios 0.9991, 1.0128, 0.9882),\nwithin 1%.\n") (parent . root) (ord . 584))))
